[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Data-Driven and Keyword-Driven Framework Architectures in Automation Testing"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As test automation suites scale, maintaining test scripts becomes a significant bottleneck if test data and test logic are tightly coupled. Advanced automation architectures like Data-Driven and Keyword-Driven frameworks resolve this challenge by separating test data and execution steps from the core test automation code."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Data-Driven Framework Architecture"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a Data-Driven Testing (DDT) framework, test scripts read test data from external sources such as Excel spreadsheets, CSV files, JSON/YAML files, or databases. The same test script executes multiple times with different sets of input data and expected results."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Separation of concerns: Test scripts contain logic, while external files contain data."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"High test coverage: Easily execute tests against hundreds of boundary and negative data values."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reusability: A single test script can validate multiple scenarios by swapping out data inputs."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: Python Data-Driven Test using Pytest"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"import pytest

# External data provider (e.g., loaded from CSV or Database)
login_test_data = [
    ("standard_user", "secret_sauce", True),
    ("locked_out_user", "secret_sauce", False),
    ("invalid_user", "wrong_password", False)
]

@pytest.mark.parametrize("username, password, expected_success", login_test_data)
def test_login_functionality(username, password, expected_success):
    # Initialize driver and navigate
    print(f"Testing login for user: {username}")
    # login_page.enter_username(username)
    # login_page.enter_password(password)
    # login_page.click_login()
    # assert login_page.is_dashboard_visible() == expected_success"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Keyword-Driven Framework Architecture"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Keyword-Driven framework takes abstraction a step further by using tables or external files to define actions (keywords) and target elements. Keywords like 'OPEN_BROWSER', 'ENTER_TEXT', 'CLICK', and 'VERIFY_TITLE' map to underlying methods in the automation engine."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"No-code / Low-code capability: Manual QA engineers or business analysts can write test cases using predefined keywords."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Modular design: Framework code is written once as keyword libraries."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Independence: Tests are agnostic of the underlying automation tool until the keyword interpreter executes them."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: Python Keyword Interpreter"},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"class ActionKeywords:
    def __init__(self):
        self.driver = None

    def open_browser(self, browser_name):
        print(f"Opening browser: {browser_name}")

    def enter_text(self, locator, value):
        print(f"Entering text {value} into element {locator}")

    def click_element(self, locator):
        print(f"Clicking element {locator}")

# Keyword Engine Executor
def execute_test_steps(step_sheet):
    actions = ActionKeywords()
    for step in step_sheet:
        keyword = step["keyword"]
        locator = step.get("locator")
        data = step.get("data")

        if keyword == "OPEN_BROWSER":
            actions.open_browser(data)
        elif keyword == "ENTER_TEXT":
            actions.enter_text(locator, data)
        elif keyword == "CLICK":
            actions.click_element(locator)"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Hybrid Framework Architecture"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In real-world enterprise environments, QA teams rarely use Data-Driven or Keyword-Driven frameworks in isolation. Instead, they implement a Hybrid Framework that combines the data-parameterization strengths of Data-Driven testing with the modularity and reusability of Keyword-Driven actions."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Maximum flexibility and scalability for large enterprise projects."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduces test maintenance overhead and accommodates changing business requirements efficiently."}]
